From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefan Hertenberger <erde74@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 14:31:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] plan9: clones close on exec, no fork while a descriptor does
 not yet, error kinds without the file name, sleep after a note, Killed, a
 pump's errors kept

From 9space's stage 3 review:
- try_clone_to_owned (every try_clone, FileDesc::duplicate, the pump's
  copy) was dup(2), which 9front makes without OCEXEC: a cloned socket
  or pipe stayed open in every child spawned later (its peer never saw
  it close, a reader no end of file). It opens #d/n with OCEXEC, in the
  descriptor's mode.
- listen and accept opened their files without OCEXEC until socket_of
  reopened them: they open them close-on-exec at once now (libc's listen
  and accept, done here). pipe(2) has no such flag: its two reopenings
  hold NOCEXEC to read, spawn's rfork to write, so no child of another
  thread gets an end (spawn's exec-error pipe: that spawn waited for the
  other child).
- error_kind matched the whole errstr, whose end is the file name
  namec adds (": 'name'"): a path with "interrupted" in it was
  Interrupted, and the callers that retry on it looped.
- thread::sleep ignored sleep(2)'s -1: a note the program carries on
  after ended it early; the rest is slept to the deadline.
- ExitStatus::code: the kernel's "Killed" was Some(1); the notes match
  without case (None, as Unix's for a signal).
- A pump's reader or writer error was taken by the first call after it:
  the next read or accept waited for ever (or WouldBlock for ever, mio
  stalled), writes went to no writer. It stays, for every call.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
